* Internal Iterators
* Patterns
  - Filtering
    +	Will return the same Type of Collection
    +	select / reject / partition
    +   Input Code Block known as "Predicate"
    	interface Predicate<T> { boolean command(T input); }

  - Transforming
    +	Input Code Block known as "Function"  
    	interface Function<X, Y>> { List<Y> command(Collection<X> input); } // for input X return all Ys
    + 	GroupBy / Flatten / Collect

  - Short Circuit
    +	Detect / AnySatisfy / AllSatisfy
    	interface Predicate<T> { boolean command(T input); }

  - Generic 
    +	ForEach

====
* Class Hierarchy of various Collections


https://medium.com/oracledevs/visualizing-eclipse-collections-646dad9533a9

This has a good tutorial on the factories to be used (both types, one with the full type and also the factories on the abstract type)

MutableList<T> list = Lists.mutable.empty();
MutableSet<T> set = Sets.mutable.empty();
MutableSortedSet<T> sortedSet = SortedSets.mutable.empty();
MutableBag<T> bag = Bags.mutable.empty();
MutableSortedBag<T> sortedBag = SortedBags.mutable.empty();
MutableMap<K, V> map = Maps.mutable.empty();
MutableSortedMap<K, V> sortedMap = SortedMaps.mutable.empty();
MutableBiMap<K, V> biMap = BiMaps.mutable.empty();
MutableStack<T> stack = Stacks.mutable.empty();

